S06a: the run record and its state machine (codeagent/runs.py).

Everything here runs against tests/unit/runs_testkit.FakeRunStore, an in-memory
twin of the Postgres store, so no database is needed. The SQL, the trigger and the
policies are exercised against a real database by tests/integration/test_runs_pg.py;
the migration text is checked by tests/static/test_runs_migration.py.

What is pinned:
  * the transition table, written out in full, and its shape (no self loops, terminal
    states final, every state reachable, every open state can still end);
  * every one of the 10 x 10 (from, to) pairs: legal exactly when the table says so;
  * the run record: id / device / profile / mode validation, collisions, per-user
    isolation, error codes (codes, never messages);
  * races: N callers cancelling at once -> exactly one winner; a state that changes
    between the read and the conditional update is re-read, not overwritten; churn
    that never settles ends in TransitionConflict, not in a lost update;
  * the parallel-run cap (welcome.limits.max_parallel_runs, ws.py), including under
    a race and for a resume from a paused state;
  * fail closed: a store fault surfaces as RunStoreUnavailable, with fixed text,
    no chained cause, and nothing but the exception's TYPE in the log.

m2c/tests/runner/test_runner_flow.py -- Patch C24: propose -> decide -> ask -> execute -> approval -> result.

Real C05 tools, real C06 policy, real C07 shell, a fake link and a fake person (``ask``).  The protocol rules pinned
here (protocol/README.md sections 2, 3 and 5; fixtures 02 and 03):

* an allowed call is answered with ``approval{allow}`` and then a ``tool_result``;
* a denied call gets ``approval{deny}`` and NO ``tool_result``;
* the person's "allow for the session" is the EXACT call, in memory only;
* an edited call is re-checked, and what actually ran is the first thing the model reads.

m2c/tests/runner/test_runner_interact.py -- Patch L01a: ``ask_user`` and ``tasks`` through the tool runner.

Both tools run on the DEVICE and, like ``plan_exit``, have no ``run_tool`` executor: the runner handles them itself
after C06 has decided and after the argument checks every tool gets.  A fake screen (the ``question`` callable) and a
fake task sink stand in for the terminal UI that L01b writes.  Pinned here:

* a run PAUSES on a question and continues with the person's answer (an option, several, free text, both);
* a decline, a timeout, a cancel and a missing screen each end the call with a fixed message and never hang;
* ``tasks`` replaces the list through the sink, writes one LOCAL event per update and answers with counts;
* nothing the model wrote is echoed into a log, and the person's answer is redacted on its way out.

m2c/tests/runner/test_runner_ordering.py -- Patch C24: idempotency, replay, cancellation, concurrency, prompts.

The shape of the rules (protocol/README.md section 5, fixture 03):

* a ``call_id`` is executed at most once, however often it is delivered, and not at all when the server has already
  closed it (a later ``tool_finished`` in the replay, or in the local log after a restart);
* cancelling kills a running shell command; a call the server closed is not answered;
* reads run side by side, anything that changes something runs alone and in arrival order;
* the person is asked one question at a time.

m2c/tests/sandbox/test_net_real_sandbox.py -- Patch C11b: the controlled network against the REAL bubblewrap.

Offline-safe: no real internet is touched.  The "internet" is a loopback server on the host that sends a banner and counts
its connections, reached only through the proxy's fake resolver and the proxy's test-only loopback flag (a constructor
argument, reachable from no setting or environment variable).  Every test checks what the command SAW and what the HOST
saw (the upstream's accept count, the policy's records), because a command can lie or fail quietly.

Proved here, for real:
* a command reaches an allowed local upstream through env proxy -> bridge -> unix socket -> proxy, and gets its bytes;
* a denied or unlisted host is never connected to; an IP literal, a private, metadata or loopback destination, a name
  that resolves to one, and a DNS rebinding are all refused even for an allowlisted name;
* the sandbox still has no network of its own: a direct connection (to an address, or to the host's loopback) fails;
* the proxy directory is read-only inside; talking to the unix socket directly gains nothing over the bridge;
* ``network="off"`` is the C11a sandbox: no mount, no proxy variables, nothing listening;
* through the shell tool: the environment (host proxy settings replaced, empty NO_PROXY), the prompt for a new domain,
  exit status and output of the command unchanged.

Needs bubblewrap, user namespaces and a system python3.  Otherwise every test is SKIPPED

m2c/tests/sandbox/test_sandbox_profile.py -- Patch C11a: the bubblewrap profile
(``m2c.sandbox.profile``).

Nothing here starts bubblewrap or needs it: ``build_profile`` only builds a list
of arguments, so every property of that list is checked on any machine.  The
system directories come from a small fake root built in ``tmp_path`` (merged-/usr
or not, with or without /lib64), the "home" is a fake directory next to it, and
every secret is a canary built at run time.

What is pinned, because each one is a way a sandbox quietly stops being one:

* the list is argv, never a shell string, and the command is ONE element;
* exactly ONE read-write bind exists and it is the project;
* ``.git``, ``.m2c`` and the shell start-up files are bound read-only AFTER the
  project (a later mount wins), and only when they exist;
* the home is an empty tmpfs; the secrets under it are never mentioned, and a
  configured toolchain directory cannot be a way to mount them;
* the network namespace is unshared and ``--share-net`` never appears;
* the environment is rebuilt from what is passed, never inherited;
* unusual project paths pass through byte for byte;
* the list of protected shell files cannot silently fall behind the policy's.

Run: cd m2c && pytest tests/sandbox/test_sandbox_profile.py -v

